WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.250
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday. Obligatory greeting from a hotel room. So last week, Sarah made a brilliant art assignment video about how

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:09.058
extremely wealthy people can sometimes use art collection as a strategy to avoid taxation.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.120
And it got me to thinking about how in general,

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:17.420
a lot of the us tax code seems to affect everyone, but in fact mostly matters only to rich people

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.200
Okay, so let's imagine four families, with two parents and two kids.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.880
Family one makes  $35,000 a year, family two

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.980
makes the median US household income of $59,000 per year, family three makes the average earned

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.840
by the wealthiest 10% of US households, $295,000,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.840
and family four makes the average earned by the wealthiest 1% of US households,

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:40.889
1.2. Million dollars. So in the US, we have these things called deductions, which allow

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:46.200
you to deduct a certain amount from your taxable income. Like many business expenses are tax deductible so if you

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:52.469
make
say $100 a year selling posters but they cost $20 to print, your taxable income would be reduced to $80. But

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:57.420
critically every taxpayer in the United States can take the so called "standard deduction" which

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:03.600
was created to simplify the tax code so that most people don't have to list their deductions they can just take the standard one.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.820
In 2018, the standard deduction for each of our hypothetical families will be

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:12.500
$28,000, or $14,000 if you're single, so no matter what each of our four families gets to take

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:17.940
$28,000 off of their taxable income, which also means that unless your other deductions exceed

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:25.560
$28,000, you don't get a tax break from them. Example: most charitable contributions are deductible in the US, but if each of our families gave

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.610
8% of their income to charity only the family in the 1%

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:36.119
who, would donate $96,000 would see their taxable income go down as a result of charitable contributions alone.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.020
Because for the rest of them their donations would be below $28,000.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:43.560
Now of course the rich family, did give much more in dollar terms to charity, but they're

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.420
also the only one of our four families to essentially receive a government subsidy in exchange for that

00:01:48.549 --> 00:01:54.029
donation. This deduction does incentivize charitable giving, among rich people and without it, charities would receive much less

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.280
money, but i think it's worth remembering that every time

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.210
you, see someone's name in big letters at an art museum or a medical research facility,

00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:06.839
35 % of that money would have otherwise gone to the government. It would have gone to build roads and pay soldiers and

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:12.629
reduce the deficit?.. probably not. Side note: you might think these tax breaks would make rich people super generous, but

00:02:12.629 --> 00:02:18.129
no. Families making between 45 and $60,000 a year on average give between 3.5

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:23.919
and 4 percent of their income to charity. Those making between 1 and 5 million dollars a year give on average between 2.8

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.220
and 3.2 percent.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:32.529
Another popular tax deduction is for interest paid on your mortgage, which is supposed to incentivize people to buy homes, although in practice it mostly

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.199
incentivizes rich people to get mortgages. To illustrate

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.609
my point, let's imagine that each of our four hypothetical families, in addition to giving 8 percent of their income to charity,

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.800
owns a home that costs 4 times their annual income. Assuming no other deductions, our family making

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.760
$35,000 a year gets no tax breaks for their mortgage or their charitable giving

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:54.640
because they're much better off with the standard deduction, as is our family making $59,000 a year. And our family in the

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:55.519
top 10%?

00:02:55.519 --> 00:02:56.019
also

00:02:56.019 --> 00:03:02.289
doesn't benefit much. Their taxable income goes from $267,000 with the standard deduction all the way

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:06.940
down to $249,000 without it. That reduces their total federal tax bill

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:12.489
by about $4,300, just over one percent of their income. Our family in the one percent, meanwhile,

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:18.548
would save over forty thousand dollars on their tax bill translating to over 3% of their total income.

00:03:18.549 --> 00:03:22.749
Now some families may have other deductions, especially around medical expenses, and I should note that home

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:29.470
ownership and charitable giving are good things for governments to encourage, but I think it's worth remembering that these tax deductions mostly benefit

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:35.259
wealthy people, and by far the greatest benefit, both in terms of absolute dollars and percentage of income, is reaped

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.680
by the richest 1% of US households.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:44.440
If you want to learn more about taxes, specifically the shocking truth about art and taxes, I highly recommend Sarah's art assignment video on the topic.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.440
Hank, I will see you on Friday.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:55.840
*sneeze* Oh that's been coming, oh, yeah i'll see you on Friday.

